WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.870
Raw pointers and references are not automatically unsafe.

2
00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:04.663
They are ordinary C++ tools.

3
00:00:04.663 --> 00:00:07.533
They become risky when their meaning is unclear.

4
00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:15.425
In modern C++ code, a raw pointer should usually communicate non-owning access unless the project has an explicit and consistently enforced

5
00:00:15.425 --> 00:00:15.784
convention.

6
00:00:15.784 --> 00:00:17.577
References also depend on lifetime.

7
00:00:17.577 --> 00:00:25.828
A reference cannot be reseated and is not null in normal use, but it still refers to an object that must remain alive.

8
00:00:25.828 --> 00:00:33.720
A reference member, cached reference, or reference captured by a callable can become a lifetime problem when the referenced object ends first.

9
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:37.307
Storing borrowed access is more demanding than using it briefly.

10
00:00:37.307 --> 00:00:43.046
A function parameter that is used only during the call is usually easier to reason about.

11
00:00:43.046 --> 00:00:51.297
A member variable, global cache, callback capture, or deferred task that stores a pointer or reference needs a stronger contract because the owner

12
00:00:51.297 --> 00:00:53.091
must remain valid for longer.

13
00:00:53.091 --> 00:00:55.602
Function signatures should make ownership expectations clear.

14
00:00:55.602 --> 00:00:59.189
Passing by value can communicate independent ownership of a copy.

15
00:00:59.189 --> 00:01:03.135
Passing by reference can communicate required borrowed access during the call.

16
00:01:03.135 --> 00:01:08.516
Passing a pointer can communicate optional or non-owning access when the convention is clear.

17
00:01:08.516 --> 00:01:12.103
Transferring ownership should use a type that says so directly.

18
00:01:12.103 --> 00:01:17.484
Good API contracts state whether the callee owns, borrows, stores, or merely observes a value.

19
00:01:17.484 --> 00:01:26.093
They also state whether null is permitted, whether the value may be retained after return, and what object is responsible for keeping it alive.

20
00:01:26.093 --> 00:01:29.680
That contract turns hidden lifetime assumptions into reviewable engineering decisions.
